\optsec{2}{The economy}
Consider an economy with two kinds of households, workers and an owner, and competitive firms; we study its stationary equilibria. Each of the $N$ workers has one hour a period, which it can sell at the wage or keep; selling it has a cost that differs from worker to worker. It receives one basket of goods and living space a period as support, whether or not it works, and spends its wage on further baskets. The owner does not work. It owns the land, provides each worker's support from the rent, and consumes what is left. It stands for whoever sustains a person outside paid work. For example, an adult may live with parents who provide housing and basic consumption while the adult decides whether to take paid work. Firms make a final good by performing a continuum of tasks, as in Zeira (1998) and Acemoglu and Restrepo (2018), using an hour of labor or machine-hours at each task open to machines, whichever is cheaper. Machines are made from machine-hours, labor, and land, the economy's one non-produced input. A machine lasts one period and nobody discounts the future, so machines earn no return; we call this case the flow benchmark. The model has no government; Section~6 adds one to tax the rent on land.

In this economy, prices are easy to determine once the wage and the rent on land are known. Each good's price equals its cost, and tracing costs through the machines that build machines resolves every price into hours and land, as in the input--output price systems of Leontief (1936) and Sraffa (1960). The main analytical issue is the wage relative to the rent. At the marginal task the wage is set against a machine whose own price contains wages. Workers compare the wage with the cost of a basket that the same costs determine, and the fixed supply of land closes the labor market.

\optsec{2.2}{Machines, land, and prices}
A machine is itself produced: from other machines, from labor, and from land, such as a site, a mineral deposit, or the ground under a plant or a datacenter. Building a machine takes $a$ machine-hours, $\lambda$ hours of labor, and $b>0$ units of land services. In the flow benchmark a machine supplies one machine-hour and then wears out. We assume $a<1$, so that machine-hours can be produced at a finite price, and $a+\lambda\gamma(0)<1$, so that at the first task the machine-hours that replace an hour of work cost less than that hour at a high enough wage.

The baseline has one non-produced input, with rental $r$ and fixed service endowment $T$ per period. Land services provide a concrete example. No activity adds to the land. Land services are used to build machines and, rented directly, as living space: a unit of space is a unit of land services. Land stands for the paper's non-produced inputs, resources whose supply the production process does not generate, such as mineral deposits. The rent $r$ is a payment for land services; it is not an interest rate.

Let $w$ be the wage, $p$ the price of the final good, and $p_m$ the rental of a machine-hour. Under constant returns and competitive free entry, each good's price equals its unit cost. If machines perform the open tasks below $x$ and labor performs the others, a unit of the final good uses $J(x)=\int_0^x\gamma(t)\,dt$ machine-hours and $1-x$ hours, so
\optdisp{p=w(1-x)+p_mJ(x),\qquad p_m=ap_m+\lambda w+br,}{(2)}
and space rents at $r$. A machine-hour costs its current inputs: the machine-hours, hours, and land services used to build the machine that supplies it. These are prices per unit. Quantities supplied or consumed per period turn them into expenditure flows. The economy is a stationary flow benchmark, with $p_m$ pricing the use of machine services. Appendix~A treats timing and durability.

The price of a machine-hour contains the price of the machine-hours used to build the machine, whose price contains the machine-hours used to build those, and so on. Substituting the machine-hour's equation into itself gives
\optdisp{p_m=(\lambda w+br)(1+a+a^2+\cdots)=\frac{\lambda w+br}{1-a}.}{(3)}
We call the tracing of prices through intermediate inputs the \emph{cost recursion}. It resolves every price into hours and land:
\optdisp{\begin{aligned}
p_i&=w\widetilde\lambda_i+r\widetilde b_i,\\
\widetilde\lambda_m&=\frac{\lambda}{1-a},\quad \widetilde b_m=\frac{b}{1-a},\qquad
\widetilde\lambda_g=1-x+\widetilde\lambda_mJ(x),\quad \widetilde b_g=\widetilde b_mJ(x),
\end{aligned}}{(4)}
for a machine-hour ($m$) and the final good ($g$), while space has $\widetilde\lambda=0$ and $\widetilde b=1$. We call $\widetilde\lambda_i$ and $\widetilde b_i$ the \emph{price coefficients} of good $i$. In the flow benchmark they are also its total requirements: the hours and land services that the production of one unit uses throughout its chain, including all intermediate rounds. The tilde distinguishes the full production chain from the direct requirements.
Appendix~B.1 gives the general production network, in which (4) holds for every good.

\optsec{2.3}{Workers}
Each of $N$ workers can supply one hour per period. A worker sells its hour ($n=1$) or keeps it ($n=0$), and consumes its income each period, neither saving nor borrowing. It consumes baskets of one unit of the final good and $h$ units of space, each costing
\optdisp{P_s=p+hr,}{(5)}
the subscript standing for support. Each worker receives one basket per period as support, in work and in exit (Section~2.4), and spends its wage on additional baskets, so that it consumes $c=1+wn/P_s$ baskets. Its utility is $\log c-\chi n$, so that the choice $n$ gives
\optdisp{u_n=\log\left(1+\frac{wn}{P_s}\right)-\chi n,\qquad \chi\ge0.}{(6)}
Consumption is measured in baskets, with one supplied through support. The parameter $\chi$ is the utility cost of the market hour, including the value of time it displaces. Non-work time is valuable and heterogeneous (Mas and Pallais 2019). The cost of work differs across workers, with a continuous distribution function $F$ and $F(0)=0$.

A worker's reservation wage $s$ is the payment that makes work as attractive as the best available arrangement without that hour's earnings. Section~4.1 derives it from support, living costs, and preferences.

\optsec{2.4}{The owner and support}
One further household, the owner, does not work and is not among the $N$ workers, so ``per person'' below means per worker. It owns the land and rents it out; at any positive rent it rents out all of it. It also holds the machines, which in the flow benchmark earn no return, so the rent is its only income. From that rent it gives each worker one basket per period, in work and in exit, and consumes $c_o$ baskets itself:
\optdisp{P_s\,(c_o+N)=rT.}{(7)}
Support is a commitment in this budget, and it is \emph{funded} when the budget leaves $c_o\ge0$, that is, when the rent covers the support, $rT\ge NP_s$.

Our participation benchmark focuses on dependence: a household or public institution provides the living standard available outside employment. If support continues during employment, earnings buy additional consumption. Here the support is funded from the rent on land. Section~6 lets the government provide it from a tax on rents, and shows why support financed from wages faces an aggregate limit as wages fall relative to rents.

\optsec{2.5}{Equilibrium}
A stationary equilibrium consists of prices $w$, $r$, $p$, and $p_m$, all nonnegative, with $P_s>0$, an assignment of tasks, and quantities at which every agent optimizes at constant prices and every market clears. Producers minimize cost and earn zero profit: each task goes to the cheaper input, and prices satisfy (2). Since $\gamma$ increases on the open tasks, cost minimization gives machines, up to a set of measure zero, the open tasks below a threshold $x^*$ and labor those above it, together with every task in $H$. We call the threshold the \emph{task-assignment margin}, or task margin. Each worker chooses $n$ to maximize (6), and the owner rents out its land and funds the support, $c_o\ge0$ in (7).

With $Y$ units of the final good, $X$ machine-hours, $N_a$ hours sold, and $C$ baskets consumed by the workers and the owner together, the markets for labor, machine-hours, land, and the final good clear:
\optdisp{N_a=Y(1-x^*)+\lambda X,\qquad X=YJ(x^*)+aX,\qquad hY+bX\le T,\qquad Y=C.}{(8)}
Hours go to final tasks and to building machines. Machine-hours go to final tasks and to building the $X$ machines that replace those that wear out each period. Land goes to living space and to building machines; it is fully used when $r>0$, and some may be idle at a zero rent. The $Y$ units of the final good are consumed in baskets, with $hY$ units of space. When $r>0$ we measure prices in land services, $r=1$, and write $v=w/r$ for the wage--rent ratio.

\optsec{3}{Replacement}
This section and the next characterize the equilibrium. Each result is stated in words with its key equation, and Appendix~A.4 proves them.

\optsec{3.1}{Prices at a given margin}
At a margin $x^*$, the cost recursion (4) prices the basket through the total hours and land services its chain requires:
\optdisp{P_s=wL_s+rB_s,\qquad L_s=1-x^*+\frac{\lambda J(x^*)}{1-a},\qquad B_s=h+\frac{bJ(x^*)}{1-a}.}{(9)}
The clearing conditions (8) give the quantities. The machine-hours are $X=YJ(x^*)/(1-a)$, so the $Y$ baskets employ $N_a=YL_s$ hours and use $YB_s$ units of land. These statements use only cost minimization and market clearing; they hold whatever the preferences of the workers and the owner.

\optsec{3.2}{The task margin}
Labor, at wage $w$, holds task $x$ when it is the cheaper input:
$w/\gamma_L(x) \le p_m/\gamma_M(x)$, i.e.
$w \le p_m\cdot\gamma(x)$. The right-hand side is labor's \emph{replacement value} at task $x$: the wage at which labor and the machine alternative cost the same there. Figure~1 draws the schedule and the margin.

\begin{optthm}{Proposition 1}{the task margin}
In an equilibrium with $w>0$ and $p_m>0$: (i) at an interior margin, the wage equals labor's replacement value at the marginal task,
\optdisp{w=p_m\gamma(x^*);}{(10)}
at a boundary assignment it is at least $p_m\gamma(x_H)$, and at a corner at most $p_m\gamma(0)$.
(ii) Holding the machine rental fixed, movement of an interior margin changes the wage at rate $dw/dx^*=p_m\gamma'(x^*)$. Where a fixed-output labor-demand schedule absorbs additional final-task hours by lowering $x^*$, a steeper capability schedule requires a larger wage change for the same movement of the margin.
\end{optthm}

Proposition~1 compares unit costs at given prices. It does not depend on how the machine rental is set, on preferences, or on the labor market.

\optsec{3.3}{Closing the replacement price}
\begin{optthm}{Proposition 2}{replacement closure}
In an equilibrium with an interior margin and $r>0$, the feedback term $1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m$ is positive, and the machine rental and the wage, relative to the rent, are
\optdisp{\frac{p_m}{r}=\frac{\widetilde b_m}{1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m},\qquad
\frac{w}{r}=\frac{\gamma(x^*)\widetilde b_m}{1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m}=\frac{\gamma(x^*)\,b}{1-a-\gamma(x^*)\lambda}.}{(11)}
Holding the other total requirements fixed, the wage--rent ratio rises with $\gamma(x^*)$ and with $\widetilde{\lambda}_m$.
\end{optthm}

The closure combines the task margin (10) with the machine-hour's price in (4), $p_m=w\widetilde\lambda_m+r\widetilde b_m$. The denominator measures the feedback from the wage into the cost of its substitute. At the margin, $\gamma(x^*)\widetilde{\lambda}_m$ is the labor share of machine-service cost. A positive rent bill requires this share to be below one. The closure is a price condition at the equilibrium assignment; household choices and market clearing also determine $x^*$ (Section~4.2).

Holding the other terms in (11) fixed, either channel lowers the wage supported by a given rent. With labor in the machine supply chain, task automation also cheapens that chain's labor. Figure~2 traces both channels from a common starting point.

When the labor requirement of the full machine-service chain vanishes and $\gamma(x^*)\widetilde{\lambda}_m\to0$, equation (11) gives
\optdisp{\frac{p_m/r}{\widetilde{b}_m} \to 1.}{(12)}
The service price is then accounted for by its embodied claim on the non-produced input. Interest and institutional rents introduce additional income components outside the two-factor flow benchmark.

Proposition~2 uses only Proposition~1 and the machine-hour's price. It does not depend on preferences, on support, or on the labor market, and it holds in any production network of Appendix~B.1 whose machine chain uses a non-produced input.

\optsec{4}{Participation and the labor market}
\optsec{4.1}{The outside option: pricing exit}
A person who declines paid work still needs somewhere to live, food, energy, and care. In this economy the owner provides that person one basket a period, and the same basket in work.

\begin{optthm}{Proposition 3}{dependence, living costs, and participation}
Work is optimal for a worker with cost of work $\chi$ exactly when the additional baskets an hour's wage buys are worth the hour, $\log(1+w/P_s)\ge\chi$, that is, when
\optdisp{w \ge s,\qquad s = (e^{\chi}-1)P_s,}{(13)}
and employment is
\optdisp{N_a=NF\left(\log\left[1+\frac{w}{P_s}\right]\right).}{(14)}
For $\chi>0$, the reservation wage rises with the cost of the basket.
\end{optthm}

Proposition~3 does not depend on technology, or on who provides the basket: a worker has one basket in work and in exit either way.

At a fixed wage, a higher basket price reduces the consumption gained from working. With support maintained, participation weakly falls. The condition depends on the wage relative to living costs, so a proportional rescaling of prices leaves the choice unchanged.

\optsec{4.2}{Replacement meets participation}
Replacement pricing and participation jointly determine the wage and task assignment. All households consume in the basket's proportions, and at a positive rent the owner rents out all its land, so the land constraint in (8) holds with equality, $YB_s=T$. With $B_s>0$, total production supports $T/B_s$ baskets and requires $TL_s/B_s$ human hours. Labor-market clearing is therefore
\optdisp{\underbrace{\frac{TL_s}{B_s}}_{\text{hours required}}
=\underbrace{NF\left(\log\left[1+\frac{w}{P_s}\right]\right)}_{\text{hours supplied}}.}{(15)}
The requirements $L_s$ and $B_s$ depend on task assignment. Together with replacement pricing in (11), this condition links participation to the equilibrium margin $x^*$.

\optsec{5.1}{What an hour's wage buys}
For every good or service, the total requirements in (4) give an exact price. A feasible alternative method also bounds that price from above. A worker can make a unit of the final good by hand in an hour, using neither machines nor land.

\begin{optthm}{Proposition 4}{the real-wage fork}
In an equilibrium with $w,r>0$, an hour's wage buys $w/p_i$ units of each good $i$, with
\optdisp{\frac{w}{p_i}=\frac{1}{\widetilde\lambda_i+\widetilde b_i(r/w)},\qquad 1\le\frac{w}{p}\le\frac{w/r}{\widetilde b_g}.}{(17)}
It buys $w/r$ units of space and at least one unit of the final good. When machines perform some tasks, it buys at most $(w/r)/\widetilde b_g$ units of the final good, where $\widetilde b_g=bJ(x^*)/(1-a)>0$ is the land the final good's chain requires.
\end{optthm}

For the basket in the same way, $w/P_s=1/(L_s+B_s\,r/w)\le(w/r)/B_s$. Combining the task margin of Acemoglu and Restrepo (2018) with the input--output price logic of Leontief (1936) and Sraffa (1960) yields these purchasing-power bounds. Caselli and Manning (2019) prove that real wages cannot fall in terms of goods produced entirely from reproducible inputs; Proposition~4 gives a lower bound from feasible human production and shows how persistent requirements for non-produced inputs constrain wage purchasing power.

The bounds hold in any production network. Where the entire production chain can be performed by a human method, let $\bar L_i\in(0,\infty)$ and $\bar b_i\ge0$ be its total human hours and non-produced services per unit of output. The barred coefficients describe this feasible alternative's complete chain, including its intermediate inputs; the tilde coefficients describe the selected method. If $\widetilde b_i>0$ (Appendix~B.1),
\optdisp{\frac{1}{\bar L_i+\bar b_i(r/w)}
\le\frac{w}{p_i}
\le\frac{w/r}{\widetilde b_i}.}{(18)}
A feasible human method with $\bar b_i=0$ gives $w/p_i\ge1/\bar L_i$. Here the final good has $\bar L=1$ and $\bar b=0$.

The rent bound counts the whole supply chain. An hour's wage buys $w/r$ units of the non-produced service; a unit of output embodies $\widetilde b_i$ units. A good with no direct requirement may still use that input through electricity, materials, or machinery. The human-method bound likewise counts its entire chain. For cloth, consider a feasible hand-production method covering the fibers, spinning, weaving, tools, and workspace. The wage and rent bill for that complete chain caps the competitive price of cloth. Cheaper machine production raises what an hour's wage can buy above the lower bound. Ownership income and transfers fund consumption in addition to wages. Appendix~B.6 gives the task-level representation of these costs.

Changes in purchasing power depend jointly on the wage--rent ratio and the requirements embodied in each good. Building taller can supply more floor space on a given site, lowering direct site area per unit. Higher crop yields can reduce the acreage needed for a given number of calories. These changes also alter requirements for structures, machinery, fertilizer, and energy; $\widetilde b_i$ counts the non-produced inputs throughout those supply chains. At given wages, rents, and total human requirements, a lower $\widetilde b_i$ reduces the price. Equilibrium affordability also depends on the wage--rent ratio. The ratio $\bar b_i/\bar L_i$ describes how strongly rents enter the human-method lower bound; ordering actual price changes requires the selected recipes.

\begin{optthm}{Corollary 1}{vanishing wage purchasing power and participation}
Along a sequence of equilibria with interior margins and a fixed $h$, suppose that the land embodied in the machine-hours that replace an hour at the margin vanishes while the feedback term stays bounded away from zero:
\optdisp{\gamma(x^*)\widetilde b_m\to0,\qquad 1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m\ge\xi>0.}{}
Then $w/r\to0$: an hour's wage buys ever less space, while it still buys at least one unit of the final good. If, in addition, the distribution $F$ of work costs is fixed, then
\optdisp{\frac{w}{P_s}\to0,\qquad \frac{N_a}{N}\to0.}{(19)}
\end{optthm}

The participation result needs the basket to remain available in exit; a public payment that favors work can keep people working along the sequence (Appendix~A.3).

The conditions concern replacement at the equilibrium margin. At fixed positive $\gamma(x^*)$ and $\widetilde b_m$, eliminating embodied labor from machine services leaves $w/r=\gamma(x^*)\widetilde b_m>0$. Human-required work can sustain a boundary assignment, and falling non-produced input requirements can sustain purchasing power. Section~7 gives a complete interior equilibrium and a sequence with a persistent hundred-to-one capability ratio across tasks.

The participation conclusion depends on maintaining the outside option. As wages buy less additional consumption, fewer people choose work when their basic support remains available. If providers' resources shrink and support falls, the participation response can change. Section~6 connects the funded basket to the income available for redistribution.

\optsec{5.2}{Factor income while labor remains employed}
\begin{optthm}{Proposition 5}{labor's share}
In the competitive flow economy with human hours and one non-produced input as the two primary factors, let $N_a\le N$ be total employed hours, including labor in machine production, and let $T$ be the fixed, fully employed service endowment of the non-produced input. Final expenditure and factor income satisfy
\optdisp{I=wN_a+rT,\qquad
\frac{wN_a}{I}=\frac{(w/r)N_a}{(w/r)N_a+T}.}{(20)}
At given $N_a$ and $T$, a lower wage--rent ratio lowers labor's share. With bounded total hours and fixed $T>0$, $w/r\to0$ implies that labor's share tends to zero.
\end{optthm}

Intermediate payments cancel when final expenditure is traced through the cost system, leaving the payments for all human hours and non-produced services. Ownership of the non-produced input receives the non-wage component of income in this two-factor flow benchmark. The wage bill includes work throughout the network; eliminating labor from machine services leaves any human hours used elsewhere in the income identity. Interest, institutional rents, and transitional capacity rents add income components outside this benchmark. Labor's share responds jointly to the wage--rent ratio and employment, so increased employment can offset a local fall in $w/r$. When the conditions for (19) also hold, both the wage--rent ratio and the employed fraction tend to zero. In the data, the labor share's decline is documented and global (Karabarbounis and Neiman 2014), and its capital-side counterpart resolves substantially into housing, which is to say land (Rognlie 2015; Knoll, Schularick, and Steger 2017).

\optsec{6}{Distribution: rent taxation and a uniform transfer}
The income identity separates two sources of purchasing power: hours sold and ownership claims. A lower wage--rent ratio increases the importance of ownership at given employment. Rent taxation and transfers determine how this income is distributed across households.

An input is \emph{terminal over a horizon} when its supply cannot expand through production within that horizon. Land is a lasting example; installed grid connections or fabrication capacity may be terminal temporarily. Electricity, structures, and extraction are activities whose input costs are traced through the system. We reserve \emph{pure rent} for income due to fixed supply and \emph{site rent} for the land component of real-estate income, excluding structures. Several non-produced inputs enter as $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}$ in Appendix~B.2. Long-run applications require persistent non-produced inputs.

We now add a government. It taxes the rental value of land, $R=rT$, at the rate $\tau_R\in[0,1]$, owed whether or not the land is used, as a land-value tax is, and balances its budget each period. A tax at rate $\tau_R$ on pure ownership rents finances a uniform per-person transfer $d=\tau_RR/N$, paid identically in and out of work. The owner's budget (7) becomes $P_s(c_o+N)=(1-\tau_R)rT$, and each worker receives $d/P_s$ more baskets in work and in exit.

\begin{optthm}{Proposition 6}{sharing rents while labor remains employed}
(i) At any positive rent the owner rents out all its land, whatever the tax rate. The physical supply of the fixed factor is invariant to a tax on its pure ownership rent. Producers continue to pay the gross competitive rental for using it.

(ii) An unconditional transfer pays the same amount in work and exit. Its direct payment differential at the participation boundary is zero, while participation can respond through income effects.

(iii) At given gross prices and hours, aggregate receipts remain $wN_a+R$, since
\optdisp{(1-\tau_R)R+Nd=R.}{}
The tax redistributes the rent component toward an equal per-person allocation.
\end{optthm}

Proposition~6 does not depend on demand or supply elasticities: the taxed input's supply is fixed, and the tax does not depend on its use.

\begin{optthm}{Corollary 2}{fiscal capacity as wage income vanishes}
(i) In the one-input economy, let $N,T>0$ be fixed and $N_a\le N$. Along a sequence of equilibria with $w/r\to0$, revenue from any wage tax with a rate between zero and one becomes a vanishing fraction of total income, while $R/I\to1$. If the basket's total non-produced input requirement $B_s$ stays bounded away from zero, that revenue also funds a vanishing fraction of one basket per person.

(ii) At given methods that use no labor anywhere in the basket's chain, $L_s=0$, and any rent $r>0$, full rent capture yields coverage
\optdisp{\kappa=\frac{T}{NB_s}.}{(22)}
It funds one basket per person exactly when $NB_s\le T$, the physical feasibility condition for the basket under these recipes.
\end{optthm}

Part~(ii) is a statement about prices at given methods; Appendix~A.3 shows that such a configuration is not an equilibrium when $F(z)>0$ for every $z>0$, as in Section~7's instance, and states the condition with several non-produced inputs.

Keynes (1930) looked forward to honoring ``the lilies of the field who toil not, neither do they spin.'' Equation (22) adds two conditions: the field must be large enough, and its rent must reach the lilies.

As the wage--rent ratio tends to zero, maintaining access to a living basket with a persistent non-produced input requirement increasingly depends on ownership income or transfers. The parents (Section~2) may themselves finance that support from wages. Support financed by another household member's wage faces the same aggregate constraint. A system funded entirely from wages becomes unable to sustain a fixed positive basket entitlement for everyone sufficiently far along the path, while labor may still be employed. A uniform rent dividend gives each person a claim on the income that remains. Broad ownership, collective funds, and other transfer arrangements can also distribute those claims. The financing constraint establishes the need for income beyond wages; institutions determine its distribution and the pace of adjustment.

Appendix~A.4 proves the corollary, and Appendices~D.2 and~D.3 develop payroll-tax incidence and rent-base coverage while labor remains employed. The fiscal lineage is George (1879); the Henry George theorem of Arnott and Stiglitz (1979) is related but distinct. Korinek and Lockwood (2026) show that in an economy without labor, taxing fixed factors is non-distortionary and can redistribute.
